consiSt("co",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Colorado Supreme Court","d":"Court of last resort. Reviews the Court of Appeals, and district-court rulings on appeals from county court, by discretionary certiorari (C.A.R. 51–55; C.R.S. § 13-6-310(4)), and hears original proceedings under C.A.R. 21. It also writes the court rules by numbered “Rule Change” orders and regulates lawyer admission (C.R.C.P. 201–227) and discipline. The court sits in three terms a year beginning in September, January, and April (C.R.S. § 13-2-101)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Colorado Court of Appeals","d":"Twenty-two judges (C.R.S. § 13-4-103). Hears appeals as of right from final district-court judgments (C.A.R. 1, 3, 4), certified interlocutory appeals of controlling and unresolved questions of law (C.R.S. § 13-4-102.1; C.A.R. 4.2), and appeals from anti-SLAPP rulings (C.R.S. §§ 13-4-102.2, 13-20-1101(7))."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District courts (23 judicial districts), plus the Denver Probate and Denver Juvenile Courts and the water courts","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts; the state is divided into twenty-three judicial districts (C.R.S. § 13-5-101). The Colorado Rules of Civil Procedure govern district courts and the juvenile and probate courts of the City and County of Denver (C.R.C.P. 1(a)). Water-court decrees are appealed under C.A.R. 1(a)(2) and 1(e)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"County courts (including small claims)","d":"County courts share jurisdiction with district courts over civil claims up to $25,000 and over eviction cases, with judgments capped at $25,000 (C.R.S. § 13-6-104). Small claims courts hear claims up to $7,500 (C.R.S. § 13-6-403). County-court civil procedure is in C.R.C.P. Chapter 25 (Rules 301–411). Appeals from county court go to the district court on the record, then to the Supreme Court only by certiorari (C.R.S. § 13-6-310)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: C.R.S. § 13-20-1101 (special motion to dismiss), effective July 1, 2019","d":"Colorado’s anti-SLAPP law is C.R.S. § 13-20-1101, added by H.B. 19-1324 effective July 1, 2019. It protects any “act in furtherance of a person’s right of petition or free speech … in connection with a public issue.” That includes statements made before, or about an issue under review by, a legislative, executive, judicial, or other official proceeding; statements in a public place or public forum on an issue of public interest; and any other conduct furthering petition or speech rights on a public issue. “Complaint,” “defendant,” and “plaintiff” include cross-complaints, cross-defendants, petitions, and respondents.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-20-1101(1)–(2); L. 2019, H.B. 19-1324","v":true},{"t":"Standard: the plaintiff must show a reasonable likelihood of prevailing","d":"A covered claim is dismissed on a special motion unless the plaintiff establishes a reasonable likelihood of prevailing on the claim. The court considers the pleadings and the supporting and opposing affidavits. If the plaintiff meets the standard, neither the ruling nor the fact that it was made is admissible later in the case or in any later proceeding, and it does not change any burden of proof.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-20-1101(3)","v":true},{"t":"Deadline 63 days after service; hearing within 28 days; discovery stayed","d":"File the special motion within 63 days after service of the complaint, or later if the court allows it on terms. The hearing must be set no more than 28 days after service of the motion unless the court’s docket requires a later date. All discovery is stayed when notice of the motion is filed and remains stayed until notice of entry of the order ruling on it. On noticed motion and good cause, the court may allow specified discovery.\nThe official C.R.S. annotations note that the special motion is also available in county court (VOA Sunset Hous. LP v. D’Angelo, 2024 COA 61).","c":"C.R.S. § 13-20-1101(5), (6); annotation to § 13-20-1101","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a winning defendant; for a plaintiff only if the motion was frivolous","d":"A defendant who wins the special motion is entitled to attorney fees and costs. A plaintiff who defeats the motion gets costs and reasonable fees only if the court finds the motion frivolous or solely intended to cause unnecessary delay, under C.R.S. title 13, article 17, part 1. A winning defendant does not get fees under this section if the claim was brought under the Open Meetings Law (part 4 of article 6 of title 24) or the Colorado Open Records Act, though fees under §§ 24-6-402(9)(b) and 24-72-204 remain available.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-20-1101(4)","v":true},{"t":"Exemptions: government enforcement, public-interest suits, and commercial speech","d":"The statute does not apply to (1) enforcement actions by the state or a subdivision, or actions to protect against an imminent threat to health or safety; (2) actions brought solely in the public interest that meet three listed conditions; or (3) claims against a seller or lessor of goods or services based on factual statements about its own or a competitor’s business made to sell, or made to actual or potential buyers (with a carve-out involving telephone corporations before the PUC). The public-interest and commercial-speech exemptions do not apply to news media, book or journal publishers, or claims based on dramatic, literary, musical, political, or artistic works.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-20-1101(8)","v":true},{"t":"Appeal goes to the Court of Appeals, but read Hinds v. Foreman (2026)","d":"An order granting or denying the special motion is appealable to the Court of Appeals under C.R.S. § 13-4-102.2, except when the trial court denies the motion because the case is exempt under subsection (8). The 2026 C.R.S. editor’s note reports that Hinds v. Foreman, 2026 CO 9, held § 13-20-1101(7) and § 13-4-102.2 unconstitutional to the extent they let the Court of Appeals review final judgments, because that conflicts with Colo. Const. art. VI, § 17 and C.R.S. § 13-6-310(1) (county-court appeals go to district court). Read Hinds before taking an anti-SLAPP appeal from county court.\nThe statute sets no appeal deadline of its own. Assume C.A.R. 4(a)’s 49 days and confirm. The annotations report that a timely motion to reconsider a denial tolls the appeal time (Said v. Magdy, 2024 COA 109).","c":"C.R.S. §§ 13-20-1101(7), (9), 13-4-102.2 and editor’s note; C.A.R. 4(a)(1)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: verified motion in the trial court, plus a copy and fee to the Office of Attorney Registration","d":"An out-of-state attorney may appear in a particular matter in any state court of record. Before appearing, the attorney must (i) file a verified motion with the trial court, (ii) designate an associate attorney admitted and licensed in Colorado, (iii) file a copy of the motion with the Clerk of the Supreme Court’s Office of Attorney Registration at the same time, (iv) pay the required fee, and (v) get the trial court’s permission.\nThe motion must list every jurisdiction where the attorney is licensed; every Colorado pro hac vice request in the preceding five years, with date, case name, number, and result; any public discipline, disability-inactive status, denied or revoked pro hac vice admission, or pending proceeding; the party represented, with confirmation the client was told of the motion; the attorney’s acknowledgment of the Colorado RPC, C.R.C.P., and other court rules; the associate’s name, address, status, and signature; and a certificate of service on all counsel and the client.","c":"C.R.C.P. 205.3(1)(a), (2) (Rule Change 2022-14)","v":true},{"t":"Colorado associate counsel must attend every appearance","d":"The Colorado associate attorney’s name and address must appear on every paper the out-of-state attorney serves and files. The associate must appear in person and, unless excused, stay in attendance with the out-of-state attorney at every pro hac vice appearance. A tribal attorney in an Indian Child Welfare Act child-custody proceeding is exempt from both the associate and fee requirements if the tribe has asked to participate and affirmed that a child may be a member or eligible for membership.","c":"C.R.C.P. 205.3(3), (8)","v":true},{"t":"Fee amount and caps on the number of admissions: not verified","d":"Rule 205.3 refers to “the required fee” without stating it in the collected text. Subsections (4) and (5) were not in any collected amendment, so whether Colorado caps repeat pro hac vice appearances is unverified. The Office of Attorney Admissions website, which posts the current admission rules and the pro hac vice application, refused automated access. Check the fee and any limits there before filing.","c":"C.R.C.P. 205.3(2)(a)(iv), (4)–(5) (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Appeals: a short update if you were admitted below; a new motion if not","d":"If the attorney already has permission in the same action in a Colorado trial court, appearing in the appellate court only requires an updated affidavit filed with the Office of Attorney Registration, with a copy filed in the appeal. There is no new fee. Otherwise, the attorney files a motion and affidavit with the appellate court clerk, sends a copy to Attorney Registration, and pays the fee as in a trial court.","c":"C.R.C.P. 205.3(6)","v":true},{"t":"Pro hac vice lawyers are bound by Colorado’s rules, but some regulatory duties are waived","d":"An attorney admitted under Rule 205.3 is subject to the Colorado Rules of Professional Conduct, except the Colo. RPC 1.15A–1.15E duties to keep business and trust accounts at a financial institution doing business in Colorado. The attorney is subject to the C.R.C.P., except C.R.C.P. 227 (registration fees) and C.R.C.P. 250 (mandatory CLE). A lawyer domiciled in Colorado and authorized to practice pending admission under C.R.C.P. 205.6 may also seek pro hac vice permission, unless employed by an entity listed in Rule 205.6(3) whose lawyers may already appear in court.","c":"C.R.C.P. 205.3(1)(b)–(c), (7) (Rule Changes 2019-03, 2021-04)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances and ghostwriting are authorized, with disclosure","d":"Under C.R.C.P. 11(b), a lawyer may (1) enter a limited appearance for a self-represented party by filing a notice before or with the covered part of the case, and withdraw without leave by filing a notice of completion; (2) ghostwrite, but every document drafted must show the lawyer’s name, address, phone number, email address, and Colorado registration number, and the party must attach the lawyer’s signed disclosure certificate stating that the document is well-grounded, warranted, and not filed for an improper purpose; or (3) help with judicial-branch forms, give oral advice, or provide short-term pro bono help without disclosure. Practice Standard § 1-1(5) implements the limited appearance, and Colo. RPC 1.2(c) requires the client’s informed consent.","c":"C.R.C.P. 11(b)(1)–(3) (Rule Change 2024(20)); C.R.C.P. 121 § 1-1(5); Colo. RPC 1.2(c) (Rule Change 2025(19))","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: mandatory for Colorado-licensed lawyers in the appellate courts","d":"All attorneys licensed in Colorado must use the e-filing system in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals. A document transmitted by 11:59 p.m. Colorado time is filed (and, if e-served, served) that day. If an attorney files on paper after being told to e-file, the court may charge $50 per document to scan it. Self-represented parties are excluded from mandatory e-filing.\nIn the trial courts, C.R.C.P. 121 § 1-26 runs the “Colorado Courts E-Filing” system. Proposed orders must be e-filed in editable format, without “(PROPOSED)” in the caption, or they are rejected. The filer must keep the signed original for two years after final resolution, including appeals. The collected text does not include the § 1-26 paragraphs on who must e-file in trial courts or how pro hac vice lawyers register.","c":"C.A.R. 30(c)–(e), (k) (Rule Change 2025(02)); C.R.C.P. 121 § 1-26(7), (15)(c) and 2017 comment","v":true},{"t":"Withdrawal needs a motion and a 14-day objection window","d":"Leave to withdraw is granted only after a motion is filed and served on the client and other parties, and either everyone consents in writing or 14 days pass without objection. The motion must advise the client of the right to object within 14 days. In the appellate courts, the client and opposing parties likewise have 14 days to object.","c":"C.R.C.P. 121 § 1-1 (as amended by Rule Change 2011-18); C.A.R. 5 (Rule Change 2022-05)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Colorado Rules of Professional Conduct (2008 restatement, amended through 2026)","d":"Colorado repealed and readopted its Rules of Professional Conduct effective January 1, 2008, in ABA Model Rule numbering, and amends them by Supreme Court Rule Change orders. The latest collected orders include 2025(29) (Rule 1.5 flat fees, effective January 1, 2026), 2026(02) (Scope [20A] and Rule 1.1 comments on technology and AI), and 2026(14) (Rule 6.5, September 2026). Colorado-only rules include Rules 1.15A–1.15E (trust accounts), 1.16A (client files), and 5.3A.","c":"Colo. RPC (Rule Change 2007-07; Rule Changes 2025(29), 2026(02), 2026(14))","v":true},{"t":"Fees: written fee and scope terms, no referral fees, no nonrefundable fees","d":"Rule 1.5 departs from the ABA text in several ways. (b) The basis or rate of the fee and expenses, and the scope of the representation, must be communicated in writing before or within a reasonable time after starting, with limited exceptions for regularly represented clients; changes must be communicated promptly in writing. (c) Contingent fees must meet C.R.C.P. Chapter 23.3. (e) Referral fees are prohibited. (f) Fees are not earned until the lawyer confers a benefit or performs a service, and advances of unearned fees go in trust. (g) Nonrefundable fees and retainers are prohibited. (h) defines a “flat fee” and provides a form agreement.","c":"Colo. RPC 1.5(b)–(h) (Rule Changes 2007-07, 2021(18), 2025(29))","v":true},{"t":"No threatening criminal, administrative, or disciplinary charges for civil advantage","d":"Colorado keeps a rule the ABA Model Rules do not have. A lawyer may not threaten criminal, administrative, or disciplinary charges to gain an advantage in a civil matter, or present such charges solely for that purpose. Telling someone in a civil matter that the lawyer reasonably believes their conduct may violate criminal, administrative, or disciplinary law is not a violation, and comment [7] gives factors for telling a notice from a threat.","c":"Colo. RPC 4.5 and cmt. [7] (Rule Changes 2007-07, 2024(06))","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct rule is broader: bias, harm to others, and an investigation exception","d":"Rule 8.4(g) makes it misconduct, in representing a client, to engage in conduct that shows or appeals to bias based on race, sex, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, religion, national origin, ethnicity, disability, age, or socioeconomic status toward anyone in the legal process. Rule 8.4(h) bars conduct that directly, intentionally, and wrongfully harms others and reflects adversely on fitness. Rule 8.4(c) lets a lawyer advise, direct, or supervise clients, police, and investigators who take part in lawful investigative activities, despite the dishonesty ban. Rule 8.4(a-2) reaches assisting a licensed legal paraprofessional (LLP) to violate the LLP rules.","c":"Colo. RPC 8.4(a-2), (c), (g), (h) (Rule Changes 2007-07, 2019(17), 2023(17), 2024(16))","v":true},{"t":"Lateral screening is unavailable if the lawyer substantially participated","d":"A firm hiring a lawyer disqualified under Rule 1.9 avoids imputation only if (1) the matter is not one in which that lawyer substantially participated, (2) the lawyer is timely screened and gets no part of the fee, and (3) prompt written notice describing the prior work and the screen goes to the affected former clients and their lawyers.","c":"Colo. RPC 1.10(e) (Rule Change 2007-07; rule text unchanged by Rule Change 2025(07))","v":true},{"t":"Marijuana, natural medicine, and AI comments","d":"Comment [14] to Rule 1.2 lets a lawyer counsel and assist a client with conduct the lawyer reasonably believes is permitted by Colo. Const. art. XVIII, §§ 14 and 16 and the Natural Medicine Act of 2022, with advice on federal law. Scope [20A] and Rule 1.1 comments [8]–[9] (2026) say technology, including artificial intelligence, does not reduce a lawyer’s duties, and list the Rules that AI use can implicate.","c":"Colo. RPC 1.2 cmt. [14] (Rule Change 2024(02)); Scope [20A], 1.1 cmts. [8]–[9] (Rule Change 2026(02))","v":true}],"style":"State of Colorado","home":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Notice of appeal is filed in the appellate court, 49 days after entry","d":"Unlike federal practice, the notice goes to the clerk of the appellate court, with an advisory copy served on the trial court within the same time. Civil deadline: 49 days after entry of the judgment or order. If notice of entry was mailed or e-served, the time runs from that date.","c":"C.A.R. 3(a), 4(a)(1), (5)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions: 14 days, and silence denies them at 63 days","d":"A C.R.C.P. 59 motion is due 14 days after entry of judgment. Any extension must be requested within those 14 days. If the court does not rule within 63 days of the last motion, the motion is deemed denied and the 49-day appeal clock starts that day without any order.","c":"C.R.C.P. 59(a), (j); C.A.R. 4(a)(3)"},{"t":"A suit can start by service, but the complaint must follow within 14 days","d":"An action may be commenced by serving a summons and complaint before filing. The complaint must then be filed within 14 days, or the service is void without notice and fees may be taxed against the plaintiff or counsel. The defendant waives the requirement by responding without reserving it.","c":"C.R.C.P. 3(a)"},{"t":"No punitive-damages claim in the complaint","d":"An exemplary-damages claim may not be included in any initial claim for relief. It can be added by amendment only after Rule 26 initial disclosures are exchanged and the plaintiff shows prima facie proof of a triable issue. Exemplary damages are capped at the amount of actual damages awarded, subject to the statute’s exceptions.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-21-102(1)(a), (1.5)(a)"},{"t":"Losing a tort case on a Rule 12(b) motion shifts the defense fees","d":"When a tort action for death or injury to person or property is dismissed before trial on a defendant’s C.R.C.P. 12(b) motion, the defendant gets a judgment for reasonable attorney fees. This does not apply if the motion is converted to summary judgment, or to a good-faith claim pleaded as seeking to change or establish the law and identifying the precedent at issue.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-17-201(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Professional-negligence claims need a certificate of review in 60 days","d":"In a damages action based on a licensed professional’s alleged negligence, the plaintiff’s lawyer must file a certificate of review for each professional named, and for any firm that employed one, within 60 days after service. The certificate declares that an expert was consulted and concluded the claim does not lack substantial justification.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-20-602(1), (3)"},{"t":"Rule 16 runs on the “at issue” date, not a scheduling conference","d":"No later than 14 days after the case is at issue, lead counsel must confer. Rule 26(a)(1) disclosures are due 28 days after the at-issue date. A proposed Case Management Order, in editable format, is due 42 days after the at-issue date and at least 7 days before the case management conference. Formal discovery generally cannot begin until the Case Management Order is served. Plaintiff’s counsel is the “responsible attorney” for these filings.","c":"C.R.C.P. 16(b)(1)–(3), (d); 26(a)(1), (d)"},{"t":"Tight presumptive discovery limits","d":"Without a court order for good cause and proportionality, each party gets one deposition of each adverse party plus two other persons (experts excluded), 30 single-question interrogatories, 20 document requests, and 20 requests for admission (plus up to 50 genuineness-of-document requests) per adverse party. Non-expert depositions are limited to 6 hours, and retained-expert depositions also to 6 hours.","c":"C.R.C.P. 26(b)(2), (b)(4)(A); 30(d)(2)"},{"t":"Expert disclosures count back from trial, and testimony is limited to the report","d":"Unless the Case Management Order says otherwise, the claiming party’s expert disclosures are due 126 days before trial. The defending party’s are due 28 days later, but no earlier than 98 days before trial if the claimant disclosed early. Rebuttal is due 77 days before trial. A retained expert’s signed report must include all opinions, sources, the fee agreement, and an itemization of fees (supplemented 14 days before trial). Direct testimony is limited to what the report discloses in detail.","c":"C.R.C.P. 26(a)(2)(B)(I), (C)"},{"t":"Pretrial-motion cutoffs: 91 days for summary judgment, 70 for C.R.E. 702","d":"Summary-judgment motions and motions to determine a question of law are due no later than 91 days before trial. C.R.E. 702 challenges to experts are due 70 days before trial, and other pretrial motions, including motions in limine, 35 days before trial, unless the court orders otherwise.","c":"C.R.C.P. 16(c); 56(h) (Rule Change 2025(12), eff. Jan. 1, 2026)"},{"t":"Every motion must certify conferral, and briefs are page-limited","d":"Before filing almost any motion, counsel must confer with opposing counsel and self-represented parties, and the motion must open with a conferral certification. Legal authority goes in the motion itself, not a separate brief. Limits: 15 pages for motions and responses and 10 for replies; 25 and 15 pages for Rule 12(b)(1)–(2) and Rule 56 motions. Responses are due in 21 days (14 if filed within 42 days of trial). Replies are due in 7 days (14 for summary judgment). An unopposed motion must say so in its title. Except for Rule 12 and Rule 56 motions, failure to file a response may be treated as a confession of the motion.","c":"C.R.C.P. 121 § 1-15(1), (3), (8), (9) (Rule Change 2024(03))"},{"t":"Untimely or missing disclosure means exclusion","d":"A party that fails without substantial justification to make a Rule 26(a) or 26(e) disclosure cannot use the undisclosed evidence at trial or on a Rule 56 motion. The 2015 amendment frames the exception as a failure that has not caused and will not cause significant harm, or preclusion that would be disproportionate to that harm, in place of the older “harmless” wording. The collected text is a redline only, so confirm the current wording. The court may add other proportionate sanctions and fees.","c":"C.R.C.P. 37(c)(1)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge found; C.R.C.P. 97 (change of judge) not collected","d":"None of the collected texts gives a party a right to remove a trial judge without cause. C.R.C.P. 97 governs motions to change or disqualify a judge, but its text was not in any collected rule-change order, so its timing and affidavit requirements are not stated here. Read the current rule before filing.","c":"C.R.C.P. 97 (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Statutory disqualification unless all parties consent","d":"A judge may not act in a case in which the judge is a party or has an interest, is related to either party within the third degree by blood or marriage, or has been attorney or counsel for either party, unless all parties consent.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-1-122","v":true},{"t":"Successor judge after trial may order a new trial","d":"If the judge who tried a case cannot finish post-verdict or post-findings duties, a successor judge is appointed. The successor may decide some duties cannot be performed without having presided, and may order a new trial on some or all issues after notice and an opportunity to be heard, stating the grounds on the record.","c":"C.R.C.P. 63 (Rule Change 2026(01), effective February 1, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Parties can jointly hire an appointed (private) judge","d":"By joint motion of all parties, filed with the Supreme Court and copied to the originating court, the parties may ask for a case-specific appointed judge under C.R.S. § 13-3-111. The motion must state the judge’s name and registration number and the agreed rate of pay, and include the judge’s agreement to be bound by the Code of Judicial Conduct as it applies to appointed judges.","c":"C.R.C.P. 122(c) (Corrective Order, Rule Change 2025(04))","v":true},{"t":"Recusal standard under the Code of Judicial Conduct: not collected","d":"Colorado’s Code of Judicial Conduct (Rule 2.11 in the ABA numbering) was not collected; only a 2026 amendment to its Application section appeared in the orders. Do not rely on this page for the recusal standard.","c":"Colo. Code of Judicial Conduct (not collected)","v":false}],"speech":[{"t":"Colorado constitutional speech protection: not established here","d":"The Colorado Constitution has its own free-speech guarantee, but neither the Constitution nor case law construing it was collected. Whether Colorado courts read it more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked. The verified speech-specific protection is the anti-SLAPP statute, C.R.S. § 13-20-1101.","c":"Colo. Const. art. II, § 10 (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Simplified Procedure is the default for cases of $100,000 or less","d":"Every district-court civil case falls under C.R.C.P. 16.1 Simplified Procedure unless it is excluded by type or the Civil Cover Sheet certifies under C.R.C.P. 11 that a party’s claims against another party are reasonably believed to exceed $100,000. A party can also move for exclusion for good cause within 42 days after the case is at issue. Under 16.1, recovery against any one party is capped at $100,000 including fees and punitive damages (the jury is not told), disclosures are sworn and fuller, and depositions, interrogatories, document requests, and requests for admission are normally not allowed.","c":"C.R.C.P. 16.1(a)–(e), (k) (Rule Changes 2018-06, 2022-01)","v":true},{"t":"County-court appeals stop at the district court","d":"A final county-court judgment is appealed to the district court for that judicial district, which reviews the record. The district court may affirm, reverse, remand, modify, or order a trial de novo. Further review is only by Supreme Court certiorari, not by the Court of Appeals.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-6-310(1), (2), (4)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Colorado Appellate Rules (C.A.R.) as stated in the Colorado Supreme Court’s official Rule Change orders: 2014(08) (restyled C.A.R.; scanned and OCR’d), 2015-06 (C.A.R. 28, 28.1, 29, 31, 32, 34), 2016-05 (C.A.R. 35, 40, 41, 42), 2022-05 (C.A.R. 1–55, major amendments), 2023-05, 2024(11), 2025(02), 2025(11), and 2026(05) (C.A.R. 3.5, 10, 21, 40, 52, 54, adopted March 26, 2026). Colorado Rules of Civil Procedure 59 (Rule Changes 2011-18, 2019-01). C.R.S. §§ 13-4-102.1, 13-4-102.2, 13-6-310, 13-20-1101 (2026 C.R.S., Office of Legislative Legal Services). No consolidated C.A.R. was collected (the free consolidated version is hosted by LexisNexis, which this package does not use). Rule subsections that no collected order restated are not described.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Colorado: file in the appellate court within 49 days, and watch Rule 59(j)","d":"Final district-court judgments go to the Colorado Court of Appeals as of right. The Supreme Court reviews by certiorari, plus original proceedings under C.A.R. 21. County-court judgments go first to the district court (C.R.S. § 13-6-310).\nTwo features surprise lawyers trained on the federal rules. First, the notice of appeal is filed with the clerk of the appellate court, not the trial court; the trial court gets only an advisory copy. Second, a timely C.R.C.P. 59 motion stops the 49-day clock, but if the trial court does not rule within 63 days, the motion is deemed denied and the clock restarts automatically.\nOnly a late notice of appeal is fatal. Other defects, including the notice’s content, are not jurisdictional (C.A.R. 3(a)).","c":"C.A.R. 3(a), 4(a); C.R.C.P. 59(j); C.R.S. § 13-6-310"},"groups":[["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal goes to the appellate court; advisory copy to the trial court","d":"File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the appellate court within the C.A.R. 4 deadline, and serve an advisory copy on the lower court within the same time. Once the notice is filed, the appellate court has exclusive jurisdiction over the appeal. Missing any step other than timely filing the notice does not affect validity, though the court may act, including by dismissal.","c":"C.A.R. 3(a)"},{"t":"49 days from entry; mailing or e-service of notice of entry restarts the count","d":"In civil cases the notice is due within 49 days after entry of the judgment, decree, or order. A judgment is “entered” when entered under C.R.C.P. 58, which makes the signing date the effective date of entry. If notice of entry was mailed or e-served to the parties, the 49 days run from the mailing or e-service. Agency-review appeals from district court also need the separate 49-day notice of intent required by C.R.S. § 24-4-106(9).","c":"C.A.R. 4(a)(1), (5), (6); C.R.C.P. 58(a)"},{"t":"What the civil notice of appeal must contain","d":"The notice must include a caption under C.A.R. 32(d) and a short description of the case (the controversy in no more than one page, and what is appealed with the basis for jurisdiction). It must also state whether the order resolved all issues including attorney fees and costs; whether it was certified under C.R.C.P. 54(b); the entry and mailing dates; any post-trial extensions, filings, and denials (including deemed denials under C.R.C.P. 59(j)); and any appellate extensions. Add an advisory list of issues, whether a transcript is needed, magistrate information, counsel information, a fee-waiver statement if applicable, an appendix with the order appealed and related findings and motions, and a certificate of service on the lower court and all parties.","c":"C.A.R. 3(d) (Rule Changes 2022-05, 2023-05)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals and joint appeals","d":"If one party timely files a notice of appeal, any other party may file one within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the original period if that ends later. Parties may file a joint notice and proceed as a single appellant. The court may consolidate separate appeals.","c":"C.A.R. 4(a)(2); 3(c)"},{"t":"Extension only for excusable neglect, and only 35 days","d":"On a showing of excusable neglect, the appellate court (not the trial court) may extend the time to file the notice of appeal by up to 35 days beyond the 49-day deadline. The request may be made before or after the deadline passes.","c":"C.A.R. 4(a)(4)"}]],["Post-trial motions and tolling",[{"t":"A timely Rule 59 motion stops the clock for everyone","d":"When any party timely files a C.R.C.P. 59 motion, the appeal time stops for all parties. It runs again from the order disposing of the last timely Rule 59 motion, or from the date the motion is deemed denied under Rule 59(j). The trial court keeps jurisdiction to decide a timely Rule 59 motion even after a notice of appeal, and appellate proceedings are stayed meanwhile.","c":"C.A.R. 4(a)(3)"},{"t":"Rule 59 deadlines: 14 days to file, 63 days to decide","d":"Post-trial motions (new trial, judgment notwithstanding the verdict, amended findings, amended judgment) are due within 14 days of entry of judgment. A longer time is allowed only if requested within that 14-day period. The court must decide within 63 days after the last such motion is filed. An undecided motion is then deemed denied for all purposes, including C.A.R. 4(a), and the appeal time starts that day.","c":"C.R.C.P. 59(a), (j) (Rule Changes 2011-18, 2019-01)"}]],["Interlocutory and special appeals",[{"t":"Certified questions of law (C.A.R. 4.2): two 14-day steps","d":"The Court of Appeals may, in its discretion, allow an interlocutory appeal from a district court or the Denver Probate Court if the trial court certifies that immediate review may promote a more orderly disposition or end the case, and the order involves a controlling and unresolved question of law. “Unresolved” means not decided by the Colorado Supreme Court or a published Court of Appeals opinion (or, for federal law, by the U.S. Supreme Court). Move for certification, or file a stipulation of all parties, within 14 days after the order. If the parties stipulate, the court must certify. Denial of certification is not appealable. After certification, file a petition to appeal in the Court of Appeals within 14 days. The C.R.S. annotations report that a motion to reconsider does not extend the 14 days.","c":"C.A.R. 4.2(a)–(d); C.R.S. § 13-4-102.1 and annotation"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP rulings: appeal to the Court of Appeals","d":"An order granting or denying a special motion to dismiss under C.R.S. § 13-20-1101 is appealable to the Court of Appeals, except a denial based on the statutory exemptions. The 2026 C.R.S. editor’s note reports that Hinds v. Foreman, 2026 CO 9, held these provisions unconstitutional to the extent they let the Court of Appeals review final judgments, in conflict with Colo. Const. art. VI, § 17 and the rule that county-court appeals go to district court.","c":"C.R.S. §§ 13-20-1101(7), (9), 13-4-102.2 and editor’s note"},{"t":"Class-certification orders","d":"C.A.R. 3.3 governs appeals of orders granting or denying class certification. The collected orders restate only parts of it, such as a joint brief limited to 9,500 words, so read the full current rule before relying on it.","c":"C.A.R. 3.3 (Rule Changes 2023-05, 2024(11))"},{"t":"Dependency-and-neglect and mental-health appeals run on 21 days","d":"In dependency or neglect cases, file the combined Notice of Appeal and Designation of Transcripts (JDF 1941) in the Court of Appeals within 21 days after entry, with an advisory copy to the trial court clerk. A cross-appeal is due within 7 days after the notice or within the 21 days, whichever is later. The record is due 42 days after the filing. Mental-health appeals under C.A.R. 3.5 also use 21 days for the notice.","c":"C.A.R. 3.4 (Rule Change 2025(11)); C.A.R. 3.5 (Rule Changes 2023-05, 2026(05))"},{"t":"County-court judgments go to the district court","d":"Appeals from final county-court judgments go to the district court for the district, on the county-court record. Further review is by Supreme Court certiorari only. The appeal deadline in C.R.C.P. 411 was not in the collected text.","c":"C.R.S. § 13-6-310(1), (4); C.R.C.P. 411 (not collected)"}]],["Record and briefing",[{"t":"Record due 63 days after the notice of appeal","d":"The record must be transmitted to the appellate court within 63 days after the notice of appeal is filed, unless the appellate court shortens or extends the time. If a court reporter’s affidavit blames the designating party’s failure to arrange payment for transcripts, that party must respond within 7 days.","c":"C.A.R. 10 (Rule Change 2023-05)"},{"t":"Briefing schedule: 42 / 35 / 21 days","d":"The opening brief is due 42 days after the record is filed, the answer brief 35 days after service of the opening brief, and the reply brief 21 days after service of the answer brief. In cross-appeals, the opening brief is due 42 days after the record, and each later combined brief (opening-answer, answer-reply) is due 35 days after service of the brief before it. The cross-appellant’s reply is due 21 days after the answer-reply brief.","c":"C.A.R. 31; 28.1"},{"t":"Word limits: 9,500 principal, 5,700 reply","d":"Opening and answer briefs may not exceed 9,500 words, and reply briefs 5,700. In cross-appeals, the opening, opening-answer, and answer-reply briefs get 9,500 words and the appellee’s reply 5,700. Headings, footnotes, and quotations count. The caption, tables, certificates, and signature block do not. A self-represented party without a word processor may file 30 pages (18 for a reply). A motion to exceed the limit must be filed with the brief. Briefs must include a certificate of compliance with the word limit.","c":"C.A.R. 28(g); 28.1(g); 32 (Rule Change 2015-06)"},{"t":"Amicus briefs","d":"An amicus supporting neither party files no later than 7 days after the appellant’s opening brief. The collected forms state an amicus word limit of 4,750 words, tied to C.A.R. 29(d).","c":"C.A.R. 29 (Rule Changes 2015-06, 2022-05); Form 6A"},{"t":"Request fees in the principal brief","d":"If attorney fees are recoverable for the appeal, the principal brief must specifically request them and explain the legal and factual basis. Citing C.A.R. 39.1 or a statute alone is not enough. Opposition goes in the answer or reply brief. The court may decide the amount or remand it.","c":"C.A.R. 39.1"},{"t":"Oral argument must be requested","d":"A request for oral argument must be a separate document titled “request for oral argument,” filed no later than 7 days after briefs close.","c":"C.A.R. 34 (Rule Change 2015-06)"}]],["Rehearing, mandate, and costs",[{"t":"Rehearing: 14 days, 1,900 words","d":"A petition for rehearing may be filed within 14 days after entry of judgment, unless the time is changed by order. It may not exceed 1,900 words, excluding material not counted under C.A.R. 28(g)(1), unless the court permits.","c":"C.A.R. 40 (Rule Change 2016-05)"},{"t":"Mandate timing","d":"The Court of Appeals mandate issues no earlier than 42 days after entry of judgment, or 28 days after denial of rehearing. In workers’ compensation and unemployment cases, it issues 28 days after judgment or 14 days after denial of rehearing, whichever is later. The Supreme Court mandate issues no earlier than 14 days after judgment. A stay pending a certiorari petition may not exceed 90 days unless extended for good cause, or unless the petition is filed and the clerk notified within the stay. An itemized, verified bill of costs is due 14 days after the mandate.","c":"C.A.R. 41 (Rule Changes 2016-05, 2022-05, 2024(11))"}]],["Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Certiorari: 42 days, or 28 days after rehearing","d":"A petition for writ of certiorari is due within 42 days after entry of the final judgment on appeal, by opinion or by order, if no rehearing petition is filed. If rehearing is sought, the deadline is 28 days after denial of rehearing, or 28 days after a new or modified opinion issued on granted rehearing. The petitioner pays a $225 docket fee when filing the petition or a motion for extension. Mental-health (C.A.R. 3.5) cases have shorter deadlines: 28 days, or 14 days after denial of rehearing.","c":"C.A.R. 51; 52 (Rule Changes 2025(11), 2026(05))"},{"t":"Opposition 14 days; reply 7 days","d":"The respondent’s opposition brief is due within 14 days after service of the petition, and the petitioner’s opposition to a cross-petition within 14 days after service of the cross-petition. Any reply is due within 7 days after service of the opposition.","c":"C.A.R. 53 (Rule Change 2023-05)"},{"t":"Original proceedings (C.A.R. 21)","d":"Original proceedings in the Supreme Court are brought by petition under C.A.R. 21. The petitioner pays a $225 docket fee and must comply with C.A.R. 12. C.A.R. 21 was amended in 2023, 2024, 2025, and 2026. Its current filing requirements were not reviewed in full here.","c":"C.A.R. 21 (Rule Changes 2023(13), 2024(11), 2025(11), 2026(05))"}]],["E-filing",[{"t":"Mandatory for Colorado-licensed attorneys","d":"Attorneys licensed in Colorado must e-file in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals. A document transmitted by 11:59 p.m. Colorado time is filed that day. Documents e-filed are served by e-service, also effective the day transmitted by 11:59 p.m. Keep the signed copy for two years after final resolution, including appeals. A paper filing by an attorney who has been told to e-file can cost $50 per document. Self-represented parties are exempt. Format and size follow Chief Justice Directive 11-01, as amended.","c":"C.A.R. 30(c)–(f), (k), (m) (Rule Change 2025(02))"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil)","d":"49 days from entry of the judgment or order (or mailing/e-service of notice of entry).\nFiled with the appellate court; advisory copy to the trial court within the same period.","c":"C.A.R. 4(a)(1), (5)","due":{"n":49,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Filed with the appellate court; advisory copy to the trial court within the same period."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal by another party (cross-appeal)","d":"14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the original 49 days, if later).","c":"C.A.R. 4(a)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Extension of time for notice of appeal","d":"35 days from expiration of the 49-day period.\nExcusable neglect required; granted by the appellate court.","c":"C.A.R. 4(a)(4)","due":{"n":35,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the 49-day period)","note":"Excusable neglect required; granted by the appellate court."}},{"t":"Post-trial (Rule 59) motion","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nExtension only if requested within the 14 days. Tolls the appeal time for all parties (C.A.R. 4(a)(3)).","c":"C.R.C.P. 59(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Extension only if requested within the 14 days. Tolls the appeal time for all parties (C.A.R. 4(a)(3))."}},{"t":"Rule 59 motion deemed denied","d":"63 days from filing of the last post-trial motion.\nThe 49-day appeal clock starts on the deemed-denial date with no order.","c":"C.R.C.P. 59(j)","due":{"n":63,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the last post-trial motion)","note":"The 49-day appeal clock starts on the deemed-denial date with no order."}},{"t":"Motion to certify interlocutory appeal (C.A.R. 4.2)","d":"14 days from date of the order to be appealed.\nOr a stipulation of all parties.","c":"C.A.R. 4.2(c)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (date of the order to be appealed)","note":"Or a stipulation of all parties."}},{"t":"Petition to appeal after certification","d":"14 days from trial court’s certification.\nFiled in the Court of Appeals, with an advisory copy to the trial court clerk.","c":"C.A.R. 4.2(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (trial court’s certification)","note":"Filed in the Court of Appeals, with an advisory copy to the trial court clerk."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal, dependency or neglect","d":"21 days from entry of the judgment, decree, or order.\nCombined notice and designation of transcripts (JDF 1941).","c":"C.A.R. 3.4","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment, decree, or order)","note":"Combined notice and designation of transcripts (JDF 1941)."}},{"t":"Record on appeal transmitted","d":"63 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nAppellate court may shorten or extend.","c":"C.A.R. 10","due":{"n":63,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Appellate court may shorten or extend."}},{"t":"Opening brief","d":"42 days from filing of the record.\n9,500 words (C.A.R. 28(g)).","c":"C.A.R. 31","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record)","note":"9,500 words (C.A.R. 28(g))."}},{"t":"Answer brief","d":"35 days from service of the opening brief.\n9,500 words.","c":"C.A.R. 31","due":{"n":35,"from":"the trigger date (service of the opening brief)","note":"9,500 words."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"21 days from service of the answer brief.\n5,700 words.","c":"C.A.R. 31","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (service of the answer brief)","note":"5,700 words."}},{"t":"Amicus brief not supporting either party","d":"7 days from filing of the appellant’s opening brief.","c":"C.A.R. 29","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appellant’s opening brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Request for oral argument","d":"7 days from close of briefing.\nSeparate document.","c":"C.A.R. 34","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (close of briefing)","note":"Separate document."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\n1,900 words.","c":"C.A.R. 40","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"1,900 words."}},{"t":"Petition for writ of certiorari (no rehearing)","d":"42 days from entry of the final judgment on appeal.\n$225 docket fee (C.A.R. 51).","c":"C.A.R. 52","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the final judgment on appeal)","note":"$225 docket fee (C.A.R. 51)."}},{"t":"Petition for writ of certiorari (after rehearing)","d":"28 days from denial of rehearing, or a new or modified opinion on granted rehearing.","c":"C.A.R. 52 (Rule Change 2026(05))","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (denial of rehearing, or a new or modified opinion on granted rehearing)","note":""}},{"t":"Opposition to certiorari petition","d":"14 days from service of the petition.","c":"C.A.R. 53","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the petition)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply on certiorari","d":"7 days from service of the opposition brief.","c":"C.A.R. 53","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (service of the opposition brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Court of Appeals mandate issues (earliest)","d":"42 days from entry of judgment.\nOr 28 days after denial of rehearing.","c":"C.A.R. 41","due":{"n":42,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Or 28 days after denial of rehearing."}},{"t":"Bill of costs","d":"14 days from entry of the appellate mandate.","c":"C.A.R. 41; 39(c)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the appellate mandate)","note":""}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Colorado Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules","s":"The rules govern civil cases in the Supreme Court, Court of Appeals, district courts, and the Denver juvenile and probate courts. County courts use Chapter 25 (Rules 301–411), and the probate and juvenile chapters control over the general rules when they conflict. Since 2015 the rules must be construed, administered, and employed by the court and the parties to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action (the federal Rule 1 wording). [Rule Changes 2015-05, 2026(11)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action starts by filing a complaint or by serving a summons and complaint. If the case starts by service, the complaint must be filed within 14 days or the service is void, and the court may tax expenses and fees against the plaintiff. The defendant waives the 14-day requirement by responding without reserving it. Unlike the federal rule, a case can begin before anything is filed. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"4","t":"Process","s":"The summons may be signed and issued by the clerk or by the plaintiff’s attorney, and must name the court and county, state the response time, warn of default, and give the plaintiff’s attorney’s name, address, and registration number. In eviction cases a represented plaintiff’s attorney must issue the summons, it must carry the case number, and it must be served with the eviction complaint form and blank answer and request-for-documents forms. Out-of-state or publication service gives the defendant 35 days to respond. Rule 4 was amended again effective March 16, 2026; only subsections (b)–(c) were reviewed here. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2024(20), 2025(25)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Since 2012 most periods are multiples of 7 days (14, 21, 28, 35, 42, 49, 63, 91, 182). Legal holidays include Juneteenth and Frances Cabrini Day (first Monday in October). The court may extend a period on request made before it expires, or afterward for excusable neglect. It may not extend Rule 60(b) times, and may extend Rule 59 times only as Rule 59 allows. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2019-01, 2022-11]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Rule 8(c) lists the affirmative defenses that must be pleaded, as in the federal rule, and also requires mitigating circumstances that reduce damages to be pleaded affirmatively. A 2015 committee comment says pleading every listed defense without a factual basis violates C.R.C.P. 11. The comment to Rule 54 notes that Rule 8(a) bars stating a dollar amount in the prayer for relief. [Rule Changes 2015-05, 2020-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings and documents","s":"Documents, including e-filed ones, must meet the paper, format, and spacing standards of Rule 10(d), and motions and briefs must also comply with Practice Standard § 1-15. [Rule Change 2024(03)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing of pleadings; limited legal services","s":"Rule 11(a) is the signature certification, quoted in the 2015 comment to Rule 12: the pleading is well grounded in fact, warranted by law, and not filed for an improper purpose. Unlike the federal rule, Rule 11(b) authorizes limited-scope work: (1) a notice of limited appearance, ending without leave by notice of completion; (2) ghostwriting, if the document lists the drafting lawyer’s name, contact details, and registration number and the party attaches the lawyer’s signed certification; and (3) form help, oral advice, and short-term pro bono help with no disclosure. [Rule Changes 2015-05, 2024(20), 2025(04)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; motions","s":"The answer is due 21 days after service of the summons and complaint unless a rule or statute says otherwise, or 35 days if served outside Colorado or by publication. If a Rule 12 motion is denied or postponed, the answer is due 14 days after notice of the ruling. A motion for a more definite statement must be made within the answer period and obeyed within 14 days. A Rule 12(b) dismissal of a tort action for injury or death triggers mandatory defense fees under C.R.S. § 13-17-201. [Rule Changes 2015-05, 2024(08); C.R.S. § 13-17-201]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may file a third-party complaint without leave within 14 days after serving its original answer; after that, leave is needed on motion and notice. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is filed. If no responsive pleading is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar, the party may amend within 21 days after filing. Otherwise amendment needs leave, freely given, or written consent. The Case Management Order sets the deadline for amendments and joinder (presumptively no later than 105 days after the case is at issue). Exemplary-damages claims can be added only by amendment under C.R.S. § 13-21-102(1.5). [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2013-12, 2015-05]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"16","t":"Case management and trial management","s":"Very different from the federal rule. The parties, not the judge, drive a presumptive schedule keyed to the “at issue” date: all parties served and all pleadings filed or defaults entered. Lead counsel confer within 14 days. A proposed Case Management Order (form JDF 622) is filed in editable form 42 days after the at-issue date and at least 7 days before the case management conference, which is held no later than 49 days after the at-issue date. The proposed order covers the at-issue date, the responsible attorney (plaintiff’s counsel), proportionality, settlement, the amendment and joinder deadline (presumptively 105 days), discovery modifications, expert subjects (one retained expert per subject per side absent good cause), electronically stored information, trial date, and related cases. Pretrial motions are due 35 days before trial, C.R.E. 702 motions 70 days, and summary judgment 91 days. The responsible attorney files a proposed Trial Management Order 28 days before trial, and deposition designations run 28/21/14/7 days before trial. Rule 16 does not apply to domestic, juvenile, mental health, probate, water, eviction, Rule 106 and 120 matters, or to Simplified Procedure cases. [Rule Changes 2015-05, 2021-01, 2022-01, 2024(13)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"16.1","t":"Simplified procedure for civil actions","s":"The default track for district-court civil cases unless excluded by case type or by a Civil Cover Sheet certification that a party’s claims against another party exceed $100,000; a party may also move to exclude for good cause within 42 days after the case is at issue. Parties may stipulate into it within 42 days. Recovery against any one party is capped at $100,000 including fees, penalties, and punitive damages, but excluding interest and costs; the jury is not told, and a larger verdict is reduced. Sworn Rule 26(a)(1) disclosures are due 28 days after the case is at issue, with extra disclosures in personal-injury and employment cases. Normally there are no depositions, interrogatories, document requests, or requests for admission. A Civil Cover Sheet (JDF 601) must accompany every initial pleading. No federal counterpart. [Rule Changes 2018-06, 2021-01, 2022-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"16.2","t":"Domestic relations case management","s":"Court-facilitated management of domestic relations cases, with mandatory financial disclosures (sworn financial statement), expert reports 56 days and rebuttal reports 21 days before hearing, and discovery completed 28 days before hearing. Amended in 2024 and April 2026; details beyond the 2012 time changes were not reviewed. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2024(03), 2026(11)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"16.5","t":"Pretrial procedure in eviction cases","s":"Special pretrial procedure for forcible entry and detainer (eviction) proceedings, amended in 2024; details not reviewed. [Rule Change 2024(08)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties; capacity","s":"A partnership or unincorporated association may sue or be sued in its common name. Both parents, or the surviving parent, may sue for the injury or death of a child and share equally, and a guardian may sue for injury or death of a ward. [Rule Change 2017-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Appeals of written, signed, dated orders granting or denying class certification may be allowed under C.R.S. § 13-20-901 (see C.A.R. 3.3). Unlike the federal rule, any order, judgment, or settlement that leaves residual funds must send at least 50% of them to the Colorado Lawyer Trust Account Foundation (COLTAF). [Rule Changes 2016-01, 2022-11]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"After a suggestion of death is served, a motion to substitute must be made within 91 days, or the action is dismissed as to the deceased party. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"26","t":"Disclosure and discovery","s":"Mandatory Rule 26(a)(1) disclosures are due 28 days after the case is at issue. They cover witnesses and documents relevant to the claims and defenses of any party, whether or not supportive, damages computations, and insurance. Disclosures cannot be objected to until the case management conference. Scope is relevance plus proportionality (2015). Presumptive limits per adverse party: one deposition of each adverse party plus two other persons, 30 interrogatories, 20 document requests, and 20 requests for admission (plus 50 genuineness requests). Expert disclosures count back from trial: claimant 126 days, defendant 28 days after (no earlier than 98 days if the claimant disclosed early), and rebuttal 77 days. Retained experts give a signed report including fees and prior testimony, and other experts give a statement. Testimony is limited to what was disclosed. Retained-expert depositions are limited to 6 hours, and draft reports and most attorney-expert communications are protected. Clawback: contest within 14 days, then present to the court within 14 days. Discovery does not start until the Case Management Order is served. Rule 26 does not apply to domestic, juvenile, mental health, probate, water, eviction, or Rule 120 matters. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2014-11, 2015-05, 2021-01, 2024(03)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"Personal service of the petition must be made at least 14 days before testimony is taken. A court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony while an appeal is pending or within 35 days after judgment. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"A deposition of anyone other than a disclosed retained expert is limited to one day of 6 hours (the federal limit is 7), unless the court allows otherwise or the parties stipulate. Objections must be concise and non-suggestive, and instructions not to answer are allowed only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limit, or present a motion. The deponent has 35 days to review and sign. A Rule 121 practice standard requires at least 7 days’ notice of a deposition unless the court orders otherwise. Rule 30(b)(6) organizational depositions were amended in 2022. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2015-05, 2016-01, 2022-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Cross questions are due 21 days after notice and direct questions, redirect 14 days after cross, and re-cross 7 days after redirect. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions","s":"Objections to the form of written questions are waived unless served within the time for the next round of questions, and within 7 days after the last questions. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories","s":"Answers and objections are due 35 days after service (the federal period is 30). Pattern interrogatories appear in Form 20. Interrogatories are limited to 30 per adverse party under Rule 26(b)(2). [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2017-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents and things","s":"A written response is due 35 days after service. Requests are limited to 20 per adverse party under Rule 26(b)(2)(D). [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2015-05]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 35 days after service, or another time set by the court or agreed in writing under Rule 29. Requests are limited to 20 per adverse party plus 50 document-genuineness requests. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to disclose or cooperate; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel need a good-faith conferral certificate, and fees are awarded unless the conduct was substantially justified or an award would be unjust. A party that fails without substantial justification to make Rule 26(a) or (e) disclosures cannot use the undisclosed evidence at trial or on summary judgment. The 2015 redline frames the exception as no significant harm or disproportionate preclusion (confirm current wording). The court may add other proportionate sanctions. [Rule Change 2015-05]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial demand","s":"A jury demand must be filed and served no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue, or 14 days after a demand for trial de novo in mandatory-arbitration cases, and the jury fee must be paid when the demand is filed. A 2012 text; check for later amendments. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A defendant may move to dismiss for failure to prosecute or to comply with the rules or orders. In a bench trial, a defendant may move for dismissal after the plaintiff rests, and the court may weigh the facts and enter judgment with findings under Rule 52. [Rule Change 2017-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"42.1","t":"Consolidated multidistrict litigation","s":"A state panel may transfer related cases to one district. A transfer motion is not entertained within 91 days before a trial date absent good cause. Responses to a show-cause order are due within 14 days, and replies within 7 days. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2022-11]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"43","t":"Evidence; absentee testimony","s":"A party may request testimony by phone or other remote means by written motion or stipulation filed as soon as the need is known, with copies of all documents to be used. The court decides whether it serves the interest of justice under listed factors. Remote appearances in residential evictions follow C.R.S. § 13-40-113.5. [Rule Change 2025(21), effective December 1, 2025]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Service requires tendering one day’s attendance fee and mileage. A Colorado resident can be compelled to attend a deposition only in the county where the resident lives, works, or does business in person, or where the court orders. A nonresident can be compelled to attend only within 40 miles of where the subpoena was served in Colorado, or in the county where the nonresident lives, works, or does business. Written objections to document subpoenas are due within 14 days of service. Rule 45 was repealed and readopted effective January 1, 2013. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2012-16, 2022-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"Jurors must be allowed to submit written questions for witnesses. The court reviews each question with counsel outside the jury’s hearing and may refuse or modify it for good cause. [Rule Change 2014-14]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court","s":"In bench trials and in rulings on interlocutory injunctions, the court must find the facts specially and state its conclusions of law separately. Findings are not set aside unless clearly erroneous, and no request or objection is needed to preserve review. [Rule Change 2017-06]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"The master sets a first meeting within 21 days after the order of reference, and parties have 14 days after notice of the report to object. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"Since 2015, reasonable costs go to the prevailing party, considering the needs and complexity of the case and the amount in controversy. Costs against the state are limited by law. Under Rule 54(c), relief is not limited by an amount stated in the prayer, because Rule 8(a) bars stating one. A judgment-revival notice requires a response within 14 days. Fee and cost hearing procedure is in Practice Standard § 1-22. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2015-05]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"A party in default that has appeared must be served with written notice of the default-judgment application at least 7 days before the hearing. Practice Standard § 1-14 lists the documents a default motion needs, including proof of valid service and a Servicemembers Civil Relief Act showing. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2017-01, 2022-11, 2024(13)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment; questions of law","s":"A claimant may move after 21 days from commencement. Unless the court orders otherwise, summary-judgment motions are due no later than 91 days before trial. Since January 1, 2026, a motion to determine a question of law under Rule 56(h) is also due no later than 91 days before trial, instead of at any time after the last pleading. Briefs are limited to 25 pages (reply 15), with 14 days for the reply (Practice Standard § 1-15). [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2024(03), 2025(12)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"When a pleading or motion challenges a statute or ordinance as invalid or unconstitutional, notice to the state or municipality must be served under Rule 5(b) within 21 days after the challenge is filed. [Rule Change 2019-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"The court signs a written judgment, and the date of signing is the effective date of entry. If a party is absent at signing, the court must immediately mail or e-serve a copy, and under C.A.R. 4(a)(5) the appeal time runs from that mailing or e-service. Entry is not delayed for costs. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"59","t":"Motions for post-trial relief","s":"A combined post-trial motion (new trial, JNOV, amended findings, amended judgment) is due 14 days after entry of judgment. Extensions may be granted only if requested within the 14 days. Opposing affidavits are due 21 days after service. The court must rule within 63 days after the last motion, or the motion is deemed denied and appeal time runs from then. This differs from federal Rules 50, 52, and 59 (28 days, no automatic denial). [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2019-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment","s":"Relief for mistake, excusable neglect, or newly discovered evidence (reasons (1)–(2)) must be sought within 182 days after the judgment or order, compared with one year under the federal rule. Other grounds require a reasonable time. Independent actions and fraud-on-the-court relief are preserved. [Rule Change 2017-01]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Execution is automatically stayed for 14 days after entry of judgment, except judgments in injunction or receivership actions. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"63","t":"Inability of a judge to serve","s":"If the trial judge cannot complete duties after verdict or findings, a successor judge is appointed. The successor may order a new trial on some or all issues after notice and a chance to be heard, stating the grounds on the record. [Rule Change 2026(01), effective February 1, 2026]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"A temporary restraining order without notice expires on the date the court sets, no later than 14 days after entry. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution and post-judgment proceedings","s":"A judgment debtor served with written interrogatories must appear before the clerk to sign and file sworn answers within 21 days. If the debtor fails, the creditor may move for an order compelling answers. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"The official forms in the appendix to Chapters 1–17A, such as the JDF civil cover sheet, case management order, and garnishment forms. Amended June 28, 2024. [Rule Change 2024(12)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"98","t":"Place of trial (venue)","s":"A motion to change venue may be filed within the time for a Rule 12(b) motion. If it is denied, the responsive pleading is due 14 days later. If it is granted, the responsive pleading is due 14 days after the case is docketed in the new court. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"100","t":"Contested elections","s":"Contests of elections for presidential elector or judicial office start with a statement of contest within 35 days after the canvass and a complaint within 35 days after that. Amended in 2024. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2024(17)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"102","t":"Attachment","s":"Prejudgment attachment. The sheriff returns the writ within 21 days, no final judgment enters until 35 days after levy, and a traverse is heard within 7 days. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"103","t":"Garnishment","s":"Writs of garnishment, including continuing garnishment of earnings, with mandatory forms and exemption notices. Garnishees answer and pay within set 7–14 day windows, and judgment debtors object or claim exemptions on the forms. Amended many times, most recently in 2020 and 2024 (forms). [Rule Changes 2017-01, 2020-28, 2024(03), 2024(13)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"104","t":"Replevin","s":"A hearing on an order to show cause for possession is set no more than 14 days after issuance, with service at least 7 days before, and the sheriff returns the order within 21 days. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"105","t":"Actions concerning real estate","s":"Quiet-title procedure. A plaintiff who, 21 days or more before suit, requests a quitclaim deed and tenders $20 can recover costs if it is refused. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"106","t":"Remedial writs; review of governmental action","s":"Rule 106(a)(4) review of a quasi-judicial decision must be filed within 28 days after the final decision unless a statute provides otherwise. The opening brief is due 42 days after the record is filed, the answer brief 35 days later, and the reply 14 days later. This replaces certiorari, mandamus, and similar writs. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2020-26]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"106.5","t":"Review of prison disciplinary hearings","s":"Judicial review of correctional-facility quasi-judicial hearings, with a briefing schedule of 42/35/14 days. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2020-13]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"107","t":"Contempt","s":"A contempt citation, with the motion, affidavit, and order, must be served personally at least 21 days before the appearance date. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"120","t":"Orders authorizing sales under powers (foreclosure)","s":"Rule 120 hearings on a motion authorizing a foreclosure sale are set 21–35 days after the motion is filed. Notice is served at least 14 days before the hearing, and responses are due at least 7 days before. [Rule Change 2011-18]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"121","t":"Statewide practice standards","s":"Colorado’s uniform local rules, organized by section. § 1-1: entry of appearance, limited appearance (§ 1-1(5)), and withdrawal on 14 days’ notice. § 1-9: notice of related cases with the first pleading or Rule 12 motion. 7 days’ notice for depositions. § 1-14: default judgments. § 1-15: motions, covering conferral certification, page limits (15/10, or 25/15 for Rule 12(b)(1)–(2) and Rule 56 motions), 21-day responses (14 within 42 days of trial), 7-day replies (14 for summary judgment), editable proposed orders, and motions to reconsider within 14 days. § 1-22: costs and fees. § 1-26: e-filing, including keeping signed originals for 2 years. [Rule Changes 2011-18, 2021-01, 2024(03), 2024(20), 2025(17)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"122","t":"Appointed judges","s":"With all parties’ joint motion filed in the Supreme Court and approved by the appointed judge, the case may be assigned to a privately compensated appointed judge under C.R.S. § 13-3-111. The motion states the judge’s registration number and pay and includes the judge’s agreement to be bound by the Code of Judicial Conduct. [Rule Change 2025(04)]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"},{"n":"205.3","t":"Pro hac vice (out-of-state attorney)","s":"Admission rule in C.R.C.P. Chapter 18. The out-of-state lawyer files a verified motion in the trial court, sends a copy and the fee to the Office of Attorney Registration, and associates Colorado counsel, who must attend all appearances unless excused. The attorney is then subject to the Colo. RPC, except the trust-account rules 1.15A–E. [Rule Changes 2019-03, 2021-04, 2022-14]","u":"https://www.coloradojudicial.gov/supreme-court/adopted-proposed-rule-changes?status_rule_change=adopted"}]});
